<p><strong>The McGillic Home</strong></p>
<p><strong>500 3rd St NW</strong></p>
<p>In 1883, a small home owned by {% person "charles-f-king" %}Charles F.{% endperson %} and {% person "estella-king" %}Estella (Cummins) King{% endperson %} was located on lots four, five, and six of 3rd Street NW. Mr. King was Morton County Superintendent of Schools (1883 to 1885), and a physician; Estella was the daughter of {% person "isaac-cummins" %}Issac Cummins{% endperson %}, a telegraph operator for {% place "northern-pacific-railroad" %}Northern Pacific Railway{% endplace %} and {% place "western-union" %}Western Union Telegraph Company{% endplace %}. They lived in the home with their children, {% person "lucille-king" %}Lucille{% endperson %}, {% person "otis-king" %}Otis{% endperson %} and {% person "lila-king" %}Lila{% endperson %}; Charles’s mother, {% person "liza-ann-king" %}Liza Ann{% endperson %}; and {% person "marion-habberstad" %}Marion Habberstad{% endperson %}, their domestic helper.</p>
<p>{% person "james-henry-mcgillic" %}James Henry McGillic{% endperson %} and {% person "john-orourke" %}John O’Rourke{% endperson %} came to Mandan from Malone, New York, and started a grocery and meat market known as {% place "orourke-and-mcgillic" %}O’Rourke and McGillic{% endplace %}. A fire originating in the {% place "northern-pacific-restaurant" %}Northern Pacific restaurant{% endplace %} destroyed their store, along with many more buildings. This fire destroyed more buildings in Mandan than any other fire in Mandan’s history to date.</p>
<p>James McGillic purchased the property in 1913, sold of the west half, and in 1916 constructed the home you see today on the east half of lots five and six. The home is 33 x 38 feet, constructed with 18 foot studs, and cost approximately $4,000.00 to build. It’s style is the Craftsman style popular from 1905 - 1930, a two and one-half story structure with a large front gabled dormer and second-story balcony over the front porch, a full-width porch with square columns supporting the roof. The interior of the house had domestic quarters, and a maid’s staircase used by the domestic help to access the kitchen. Quarter-sawn oak is used extensively throughout the main floor for the woodwork, wainscot, and built-in china hutch. There are four bedrooms and one full- and two half-baths.</p>
